  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "For a differentiable function, what is usually true of the derivative at a local extremum?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "It must be greater than \\(0\\)",
    "B": "It equals \\(0\\)",
    "C": "It must be less than \\(0\\)",
    "D": "It must not exist"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "At an interior local extremum of a differentiable function, a necessary condition is that the derivative equals \\(0\\), so the answer is B. Conversely, a derivative of \\(0\\) does not necessarily indicate a local extremum. Opposite derivative signs throughout adjacent left and right neighborhoods give a sufficient test for an extremum, but this test is not necessary for every differentiable extremum. One-sided endpoint extrema need not have zero derivative.",
